id: decisions/028-consent-before-crossing-a-boundary
title: "Consent before any effect crosses a boundary"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'consent-timing'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: consent-timing
axis_gloss: "whether agreement is obtained before the effect lands, or the effect lands first and is reviewed or corrected afterwards"
poles:
  a: consent-before-effect
  b: act-then-audit
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 10.1
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(12) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Must agreement come before an effect lands in every case, or may work be
  staged first — a browser-local draft, a private draft PR, a local render, a
  database row flagged for review — as long as nothing crosses to another
  person, the public or canonical state without consent?

why_it_matters: >
  The three authored rules (normalize before a record exists, partner review
  before relationship moves, offer before installing) all guard an effect that
  reaches someone else, while every act-first artifact stages inside a private
  or local boundary and pauses before promotion; two sibling interface contracts
  even disagree on whether browser-local autosave counts as forbidden
  persistence. Once answered, a bot knows which of its steps may run unattended
  and exactly which step is the gate, instead of reading 'human review' as
  either always-first or always-after.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    8 judgments sit at consent-before-effect (3 authored) and 4 at act-then-audit
    (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave counts a browser-local draft or a private draft PR as an effect
    at all, and whether a needs-review row in the feedback database is already
    canonical to him.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: No effect of any kind lands before consent. Nothing is saved, staged,
      rendered or inserted until a human has agreed; review is the first step, not
      the ninth.
    cost: "Removes autosave from interface/new-yawn-v0.1.yawn, reorders database/feedback-intake.yawn status_flow, moves the human pause in interface/local-observation-art-v0.1.yawn before rendering, and contradicts the outcome-attached feedback loop that stages a private draft PR before notifying Dave."
    risk: "Every loop blocks on a human before it has anything to show; the local art contract's 'render under a bounded grant, promote separately' design is lost."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/local-observation-art-contract.test.mjs to assert pause-for-human-review precedes render-through-local-comfyui; today the reverse is asserted and passes."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0077"
      - "J0380"
      - "J0425"
      - "J0444"
      - "J0506"
      - "J0620"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Effects land first and are audited after. Review is a stage that follows
      staging, insertion or rendering, and consent is corrective rather than prior.
    cost: "Contradicts examples/relationship-commitment-conversation.yawn (partner review blocks the move, stated three times) and interface/desktop-homebase-v1.yawn (offer, never silent install)."
    risk: "A relationship-impacting move or a shell install lands before the other person has agreed; 'no hidden agent authority' in core/design-principles.yawn v0_lock is breached."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "None can enforce audit-after; the record would be striking must_ask_first from the relationship example and the update-offer line from desktop-homebase."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0341"
      - "J0486"
      - "J1044"
      - "J1048"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Consent comes before any effect that crosses a boundary: reaches another
      person, the public, canonical state, spend, or a device outside the owner's
      control. Inside a private, local and reversible boundary (browser-local draft,
      private draft PR, local render, a row flagged needs-review that is not
      canonical) work may be staged first and reviewed after, and every such surface
      names the boundary it stays inside. Consequences named:
      interface/yawn-observation-view-v1.yawn's forbidden automatic_persistence is
      qualified to canonical or remote persistence so new-yawn's browser-local
      autosave is not in conflict; database/feedback-intake.yawn says rows before
      needs-review are not canonical.
    cost: "One sentence in core/proof-and-boundary.yawn or design-principles v0_lock; a qualifier on one interface contract; a placement note on one database contract."
    risk: "'Private boundary' widens over time until a draft PR becomes a merged PR."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs: any contract declaring autosave or automatic creation must also declare persistence: browser-local (or equivalent), live_model_calls: none and typing_network_requests: none; extend tests/local-observation-art-contract.test.mjs to assert separate_human_decision is the last entry of order: and promote-through-a-separate-approval the last of pipeline:."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0077"
      - "J0380"
      - "J0425"
      - "J0444"
      - "J1044"
      - "J1048"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.75
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    All authored judgments are consent-first and all act-first judgments are
    revealed, but the revealed ones share one shape: staging inside a boundary the
    owner controls, with the crossing step gated after. The test suite already
    implements exactly that split ('rendering requires a bounded local grant and
    cannot promote itself'; 'the recurring contract can propose but cannot
    generate, spend, or publish'), and the outcome-attached feedback loop (private
    draft PR, then notify, then review) is this rule in practice. The one real
    conflict is between two sibling interface contracts over what 'persistence'
    means, which C resolves by qualifying the word.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "The boundary-crossing rule is written once in core/proof-and-boundary.yawn or core/design-principles.yawn v0_lock."
    - "interface/yawn-observation-view-v1.yawn and interface/new-yawn-v0.1.yawn no longer disagree on automatic persistence, and a test asserts autosave surfaces declare their local boundary."
    - "database/feedback-intake.yawn states that rows before needs-review are not canonical."
    - "tests/local-observation-art-contract.test.mjs asserts the human decision is the last ordered step and passes."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A surface that can send, publish, spend or write canonical state gains an
    automatic step before its consent gate and npm test stays green.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0077"
  - "J0380"
  - "J0425"
  - "J0444"
  - "J0506"
  - "J0620"
  - "J0341"
  - "J0486"
  - "J1044"
  - "J1048"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
